            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Civil procedure<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    The appellant suggests that the <b>jurisdiction</b> of the Federal Court, based on s. 31.1 of the Act, and that of the Superior Court, resulting from its general common law <b>jurisdiction</b>, are different. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;For example, if a party decides to claim the sum of $20,000 for contractual fault both in the Superior Court and in the <b>Small Claims</b> Court, there will be no basis for invoking lis pendens since the <b>Small Claims</b> Court does not have the necessary <b>jurisdiction</b> to render a judgment in such a matter. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;[TRANSLATION] . . . if one of the courts concerned lacks <b>jurisdiction</b>, the question must be resolved by an exception as to <b>jurisdiction</b>, not by a declinatory exception of lis pendens or connexity. 
                </p>
