                        - <span class="citation">2003 SCC 55</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2003] 2 SCR 585</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2003-10-03</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Administrative law<br/>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    Second, even if the statement in Pembina does not embrace the Commission, La Forest J. was speaking of the <b>jurisdiction</b> of a <b>small claims</b> court, and I do not think he can be taken to have been pronouncing, by implication, on broader questions. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;I do not see how, by raising a defence of aboriginal right, a respondent should be able to alter the primary <b>jurisdiction</b> of the Commission or halt its proceedings. The nature of a particular defence should be seen as secondary to the Commission’s primary <b>jurisdiction</b>. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;These bodies were found, by the chambers judge, to have no <b>jurisdiction</b> to determine whether a s. 35 right could be invoked. It is unnecessary for the purposes of this appeal to pronounce upon the <b>jurisdiction</b> of the District Manager and the Administrative Review Panel. 
                </p>
