        </div> 
    </div> 
    </div> 
</li>
    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">15.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/1882/index.do?q=%22Small+Claims%22+jurisdiction">Danyluk v. Ainsworth Technologies Inc.</a></span>
                        - <span class="citation">2001 SCC 44</span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[2001] 2 SCR 460</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">2001-07-12</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Administrative law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    It is clear that an administrative decision which is made without <b>jurisdiction</b> from the outset cannot form the basis of an estoppel. Where an administrative officer or tribunal initially possessed the <b>jurisdiction</b> to make a decision in a judicial manner but erred in the exercise of that <b>jurisdiction</b>, the resulting decision &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;Although she had <b>jurisdiction</b> under the ESA to deal with the claim, the ESA officer lost <b>jurisdiction</b> when she failed to disclose to the appellant the case the appellant had to meet and to give the appellant the opportunity to be heard in answer to the case put against her. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;51 In summary, it is clear that an administrative decision which is made without <b>jurisdiction</b> from the outset cannot form the basis of an estoppel. The conditions precedent to the adjudicative <b>jurisdiction</b> must be satisfied. 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
        <div class="documents">
