    <li class="even list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">22.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/234/index.do?q=%22Small+Claims%22+jurisdiction">Ontario (Attorney General) v. OPSEU</a></span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[1987] 2 SCR 2</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">1987-07-29</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    It must be recalled that the "pith and substance" doctrine, exemplified by Bank of Toronto v. Lambe, is that a law which is in relation to a matter within <b>jurisdiction</b> ... is not objectionable just because it affects a matter outside <b>jurisdiction</b> . . . . &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;(1) federal and provincial governments have different areas of <b>jurisdiction</b>, and federal and provincial elections raise different political issues. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;The plain fact is that even in a federation with divided <b>jurisdictions</b>, the object of the political discourse, the ultimate form of political activity, remains indivisible. 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
