    <li class="even list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">24.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/5697/index.do?q=%22Small+Claims%22+jurisdiction">Dauphin Plains Credit Union Ltd. v. Xyloid Industries Ltd.</a></span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[1980] 1 SCR 1182</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">1980-03-18</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Contract<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    In The Payment of Wages Act as in The Employment Standards Act the legislature was exclusively concerned with matters within its <b>jurisdiction</b>. In respect of deductions, minimum wage orders are concerned only with those which are under the control of the provincial legislature, they make no reference to the deductions &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;It is therefore clear that the receiver had ample funds to cover those two <b>small claims</b>. For those reasons I would allow the appeal only to the extent of deducting from the amount allowed by the judgment of the Court of Appeal 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
        <div class="documents">
                <a target="_blank" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/5697/1/document.do" title="Download the PDF version" class="matomo_download"><span class="decisia-icon icon-decisia-file-pdf"></span></a>
                <a href='javascript:void(0)' class='expand-result' title='Collapse result'></a>
        </div> 
    </div> 
    </div> 
</li>
    <li class="odd list-item-expanded">
<div class="number">
    <span class="digits">25.</span>
</div>
<div class="metadata">
    <div class="info" >
        <div class="subinfo">
            <h3>
                <span class="title"><a target="_parent" href="/scc-csc/scc-csc/en/item/1287/index.do?q=%22Small+Claims%22+jurisdiction">R. v. Pontes</a></span>
                    - <span class="report-citation">[1995] 3 SCR 44</span>
                - <span class="publicationDate">1995-09-21</span>
            </h3>
            <div class="subMetadata">
                <span>Supreme Court Judgments</span>
            </div> 
            <div class="subject">
                    <span>
                            Constitutional law<br/>
                            Criminal law<br/>
                    </span>
            </div> 
                <p class="snippet" >
                    72. (1) Subject to subsection (6), but notwithstanding any other provision of this Act, any other Act, regulation, municipal bylaw or order made by a justice, no justice shall, except under the <b>Small Claims</b> Act, order that a person be imprisoned by reason only that he defaults in paying a fine. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp; to comply with this Act or the regulations", because the person "has a driving record that in the opinion of the superintendent is unsatisfactory", where the person's license has been suspended in another Canadian or American <b>jurisdiction</b>, or for any other cause "that relates to the use or operation of motor vehicles". &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;The latter situation is evidenced in <b>jurisdictions</b> where provision is made for the clerk of the court, the presiding magistrate or some other official giving notice to the accused of the suspension of his licence before that suspension can be effective. 
                </p>
                <div class="spacer">
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                        <span class="decisia-icon"></span>
                </div>
        </div> 
        <div class="documents">
